      {
        "id": "p16-q48-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why $P(x)$ must be a quadratic.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For a nonconstant polynomial of degree $n$, $P(x+1)-P(x)$ has degree exactly $n-1$. Since $6x+4$ has degree $1$, $n=2$.",
          "worked": "The nonzero difference shows that $P(x)$ is nonconstant. Let its degree be $n$ and its leading term be $kx^n$, where $k\\ne0$.\n\nIn $P(x+1)-P(x)$, the $x^n$ terms cancel. The leading remaining term is $knx^{n-1}$, whose coefficient is nonzero. Hence the difference has degree exactly $n-1$.\n\nSince $6x+4$ has degree $1$, $n-1=1$, so $n=2$. Therefore $P(x)$ is quadratic.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q48-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, find the polynomial.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(x)=3x^2+x+3$.\n\nSubstitute $P(x)=ax^2+bx+c$ into the difference identity and equate coefficients; use $P(0)=3$ to find $c$.",
          "worked": "Let $P(x)=ax^2+bx+c$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x+1)-P(x)&=a(x+1)^2+b(x+1)+c-(ax^2+bx+c)\\\\P(x+1)-P(x)&=2ax+a+b.\\end{align*}$$\nEquating coefficients with $6x+4$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}2a&=6\\\\a&=3\\\\a+b&=4\\\\b&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nAlso,\n$$\\begin{align*}P(0)&=c=3\\\\P(x)&=3x^2+x+3.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      }
    ]
